{:edb/db-before-t 1985 :edb/db-after-t 1986 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535298 50 #inst "2026-10-08T21:33:08.771Z" 13194139535298 true] [17592186175760 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/assets/ffaf242238a8d32ce0f134cff6e0cf4256b2888b0a71f65e3c5ec470cbce284a.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above and the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle 2y + \\ln{(\\sqrt{x+5})}, \\: e^{y/3} + 3x \\right\\rangle.$ \nFind a repeated integral in plane polar coordinates that's equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r} \\, .$" 13194139535298 true] [17592186175760 1100 "Consider the path $C$ shown above and the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle 2y + \\ln{(\\sqrt{x+5})}, \\: e^{y/3} + 3x \\right\\rangle$.\nFind a repeated integral in plane polar coordinates that's equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r} \\,$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3627/Source/Images/f328d63e617baaed38300a4f68a3df63.png)" 13194139535298 false] [17592186175760 1145 "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}} = \\oint\\limits_{C} (2y + \\ln{(\\sqrt{x+5})}) \\: \\mathrm{d}x + \\left(e^{y/3} + 3x \\right) \\: \\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y = \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3627/Source/Images/e8dfd43252a2b65794b5a03ca7b3995d.png)\n\nSince\n\n$$\nP(x,y)= 2y + \\ln{(\\sqrt{x+5})}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x,y)=e^{y/3} + 3x\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y} = 2, \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} = 3\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)(2y + \\ln (\\sqrt{x + 5}))dx + (e^{y/3} + 3x)dy &= ∬_(D)(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} - \\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(3 - 2)dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{(r,\\theta) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 3, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi \\right\\}\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\: r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)dA &= ∬_(Δ)rdrdθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{π/2}^{2π}∫_{0}^{3}rdrdθ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535298 false] [17592186175760 1145 "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}}\n=\n\\oint\\limits_{C} (2y + \\ln{(\\sqrt{x+5})}) \\: \\mathrm{d}x + \\left( e^{y/3} + 3x \\right) \\: \\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/assets/e4c7913793a4b08916a832e575c5b2c167d5dfeec244eea23857731df1e514e7.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)= 2y + \\ln{(\\sqrt{x+5})}$ and $Q(x,y)=e^{y/3} + 3x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(2y+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x+5}))\\,dx+({e}^{y/3}+3x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3-2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 3, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535298 true] [17592186175801 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/assets/e12a47b603d2c4193453d0f4daf18f54e52ed973498f520feef32cfafbe28a9f.png)\n\n  \n\nEvaluate ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/assets/696a0cf0c1864bfb945b4321a81525094319c9aa23491929e11dcd2b4bf2e842.png) where the path ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/assets/41f045554bfb2c134d481db3d019376c7427e136ebd890f5537789d0c04be67d.png) is shown above." 13194139535298 true] [17592186175801 1100 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_{C} \\left(e^{-x^2}-y^3\\right) \\mathrm{d}x + (e^{-y^2}+x^3) \\: \\mathrm{d}y$, where the path $C$ is shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3627/Source/Images/2e2cf25681d84163eb1887aebdea07b0.png)" 13194139535298 false] [17592186175801 1145 "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y = \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3627/Source/Images/80b659f9413d74a0171fcf45372ad34d.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = e^{-x^2}-y^3, \\qquad Q(x,y) = e^{-y^2}+x^3\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y} =-3y^{2}, \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} = 3x^{2}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)(e^{-x^{2}} - y^{3})dx + (e^{-y^{2}} + x^{3})dy &= ∬_(D)(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} - \\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(3x^{2} - (-3y^{2}))dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)3(x^{2} + y^{2})dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{(r,\\theta) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi \\Big\\}\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\: r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)(3x^{2} + y^{2})dA &= ∬_(Δ)3(r^{2}\\cos^{2} θ + r^{2}\\sin^{2} θ)rdrdθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}∫_{0}^{2}3r^{3}(\\cos^{2} θ + \\sin^{2} θ)drdθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}∫_{0}^{2}3r^{3}drdθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}[\\frac{3r^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{2}dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}12dθ \\\\\n&= 12[θ]_{0}^{π} \\\\\n&= 12π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535298 false] [17592186175801 1145 "Green's theorem states that\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/assets/84bdabd8bc9134d577c789d22aa75d8df2b13646db10e6af742a9eba2bd0c6eb.png)\n\nIn our case, the region ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/assets/28390f00cdb34a228868c36035ff66f1ea65bf7ff5e592fa473fca18d6addbcc.png) and its boundary ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061894/assets/54c5a03048971806da4fc3eb5f51c9fdefb00abbb4a54b7bf9b94ba81ab0df37.png) satisfy the conditions of Gre